      "prompt": "You are now consulting as **eric-ries** (Domain Expert).\n\nBased on this expert's knowledge and frameworks, provide a focused recommendation.\n\n## Expert's Key Frameworks\n- **Experiment Design (Concierge MVP):**: Recruit 5 remote teams manually\n- **Step 1 — Baseline (where you started):**: Trial-to-paid: 2.1% | Day-30 retention: 8% | NPS: 12\n- **Step 2 — Tuning (last 3 months of experiments):**: Experiment 1 (onboarding redesign): Trial-to-paid → 2.4% (+0.3pp)\n- **Customer Need Pivot**: your users have a problem, just not\n- **Validation & MVP:**: `*validate-idea {idea} [market]` - Validate using Value + Growth Hypothesis\n\n## Expert's Core Principles\n(see full context below)\n\n## Recent Knowledge (from HYDRA feed, last 30 days)\n\n### [2026-05-15] [Tier S] AI/ML Powers Health: Build with ML Kit & Med-Gemma\n**Source:** https://dev.to/sushan_dristi_ab98c07ea8f/aiml-powers-health-build-with-ml-kit-med-gemma-40l2\n**Key Insights:** - AI and ML are transforming healthcare by enhancing diagnosis and personalizing treatment.\n- ML Kit provides APIs for mobile developers to integrate AI capabilities into healthcare applications.\n- Med-Gemma is an advanced large language model designed for complex medical language understanding.\n- AI can forecast disease outbreaks and predict patient deterioration.\n- Data privacy and security are critical challenges in integrating AI/ML into healthcare.\n---\n### [2026-05-15] [Tier S] Mechanistic Interpretability of EEG Foundation Models via Sparse Autoencoders\n**Source:** https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.13930\n**Key Insights:** - EEG foundation models achieve state-of-the-art clinical performance but their internal computations remain opaque, creating a barrier to clinical trust\n- The research applied TopK Sparse Autoencoders across three architecturally distinct EEG transformers: SleepFM, REVE, and LaBraM\n- Features were grounded in a clinical taxonomy consisting of abnormality, age, sex, and medication categories\n- A single hyperparameter procedure driven by dictionary health audit transfers robustly across all three architectures\n- Concept steering reveals three operational regimes: selectively steerable, encoded but entangled, and non-encoded\n- The framework exposes 'wrecking-ball' interventions that collapse global model performance\n- Clinical entanglements exist where age-pathology confounding makes it impossible to suppress one concept without corrupting the other\n- A spectral decoder translates latent manipulations into physiologically interpretable frequency signatures including pathological slow-wave suppression and alpha-band restoration\n---\n### [2026-05-15] [Tier S] Beyond Mode-Seeking RL: Trajectory-Balance Post-Training for Diffusion Language Models\n**Source:** https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.13935\n**Key Insights:** - Diffusion language models suffer from 'trajectory locking' during reward-driven post-training, where probability mass over-concentrates onto narrow denoising paths, reducing coverage of alternative correct solutions\n- TraFL (Trajectory Flow baLancing) uses a trajectory-balance objective that trains toward a reward-tilted target distribution anchored to a frozen reference model\n- TraFL makes trajectory-balance practical for diffusion language models by using a diffusion-compatible sequence-level surrogate and learned prompt-dependent normalization\n- TraFL is the only evaluated post-training method that improves over the base model in every benchmark-length setting, with gains persisting as sampling budget increases\n- TraFL's improvements transfer to held-out evaluations, outperforming on Minerva Math and across all LiveCodeBench difficulty splits\n- Current post-training methods for diffusion language models largely adapt reward-maximizing objectives, which may not be optimal\n---\n### [2026-05-15] [Tier S] EvolveMem:Self-Evolving Memory Architecture via AutoResearch for LLM Agents\n**Source:** https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.13941\n**Key Insights:** - Existing LLM agent memory systems have a critical limitation: they keep retrieval infrastructure fixed while only allowing stored content to evolve\n- EvolveMem implements a two-level co-evolution approach that adapts both stored knowledge and the retrieval mechanism simultaneously\n- The system uses an LLM-powered diagnosis module that analyzes failure logs to identify root causes and propose configuration adjustments\n- EvolveMem includes safety mechanisms with automatic rollback and exploration triggers to prevent performance degradation\n- The system can autonomously discover new configuration dimensions that were not part of the original design\n- EvolveMem achieves 25.7% relative improvement over the strongest baseline on LoCoMo benchmark\n- The system shows 78.0% relative improvement over minimal baseline configuration on LoCoMo\n- EvolveMem achieves 18.9% relative improvement over strongest baseline on MemBench benchmark\n- Evolved configurations demonstrate positive cross-benchmark transfer, suggesting the system learns universal retrieval principles rather than benchmark-specific tricks\n- EvolveMem implements an AutoResearch process where the system conducts autonomous iterative research cycles on its own architecture\n---\n### [2026-05-15] [Tier S] A Unified Geometric Framework for Weighted Contrastive Learning\n**Source:** https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.13943\n**Key Insights:** - Weighted InfoNCE objectives can be interpreted as Distance Geometry Problems where the weighting scheme specifies the target geometry of the representation\n- Both SupCon and Soft SupCon collapse samples within each class to a single prototype in supervised classification\n- Balanced SupCon recovers classical regular simplex geometry, but class imbalance breaks this symmetry and induces non-uniform inter-class similarities\n- Soft SupCon preserves regular simplex geometry regardless of class imbalance, unlike standard SupCon\n- y-Aware CL cannot attain its entropic optimum unless labels lie on a hypersphere, revealing a fundamental geometric mismatch\n- Geometrically consistent weighting choices like Euclidean-Euclidean weighting or X-CLR admit unique optimal embeddings\n- The choice of weighting scheme determines whether contrastive learning is geometrically realizable, degenerate, or inconsistent\n- Contrastive learning aims to preserve relational structure between samples by learning representations that reflect a similarity graph\n---\n### [2026-05-15] [Tier S] Towards Resource-Efficient LLMs: End-to-End Energy Accounting of Distillation Pipelines\n**Source:** https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.13981\n**Key Insights:** - Distillation claims rarely account for full end-to-end energy costs including teacher-side workloads such as data generation, logit caching, and evaluation\n- The research presents a comprehensive energy accounting framework that measures complete computational cost via stage-wise tracking of GPU device power consumption\n- Two common distillation methods are examined: classic logit-based knowledge distillation and synthetic-data supervised fine-tuning\n- The study constructs energy-quality Pareto frontiers that expose previously ignored costs in distillation processes\n- The research derives practical design rules for selecting distillation methods and hyperparameters under energy and budget constraints\n- An open-source measurement harness and accounting protocol is released to standardize reproducible distillation research with explicit energy accountability\n- The rise in LLM deployment has driven GPU demand and datacenter scaling, raising concerns about electricity use and grid stress\n---\n### [2026-05-15] [Tier S] TabPFN-3: Technical Report\n**Source:** https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.13986\n**Key Insights:** - TabPFN-3 scales to datasets with 1M training rows and substantially reduces training and inference time compared to previous versions\n- TabPFN-3 is pretrained exclusively on synthetic data from the model's prior, not on real-world data\n- A single forward pass of TabPFN-3 outperforms all other models on TabArena benchmark, including tuned and ensembled baselines\n- TabPFN-3 beats 8-hour-tuned gradient-boosted-tree baselines on datasets up to 1M training rows and 200 features\n- TabPFN-3 introduces test-time compute scaling as a novel capability for tabular foundation models\n- TabPFN-3-Plus (Thinking) beats all non-TabPFN models by over 200 Elo on TabArena, with the advantage increasing to 420 Elo on largest datasets\n- TabPFN-3-Plus outperforms AutoGluon 1.5 extreme while being 10x faster, without using LLMs, real data, internet search or other models\n- TabPFN-3 achieves new state-of-the-art performance on relational data benchmark RelBenchV1\n- TabPFN-3 achieves state-of-the-art on tabular-text data via TabPFN-3-Plus on the TabSTAR benchmark\n- The specialized time-series checkpoint TabPFN-TS-3 ranks 2nd on the fev-bench time-series benchmark\n- SHAP-value computation in TabPFN-3 is up to 120x faster than in previous versions\n- TabPFN-3 is up to 20x faster than TabPFN-2.5\n- A reduced KV cache and row-chunking enable TabPFN-3 to scale to 1M rows on one H100 GPU with fast inference\n---\n### [2026-05-15] [Tier S] HodgeCover: Higher-Order Topological Coverage Drives Compression of Sparse Mixture-of-Experts\n**Source:** https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.13997\n**Key Insights:** - Existing sparse MoE compressors are structurally blind to triplet compatibility because they only use pairwise signals to rank experts\n- The obstruction to merging experts is mathematically represented by the harmonic kernel of the simplicial Laplacian on a 2-complex\n- Hodge decomposition of the edge-barrier signal can isolate the harmonic kernel exactly\n- HodgeCover operates by greedily covering harmonic-critical edges and triplet-critical triangles\n- HodgeCover can be combined with weight pruning in a hybrid approach for improved compression\n- HodgeCover matches state-of-the-art learning-free baselines on expert reduction and leads on aggressive compression with the hybrid variant\n- HodgeCover uniquely balances retained mass across all four Hodge components\n- Sparse MoE layers can be compressed without retraining using learning-free methods\n---\n### [2026-05-15] [Tier S] Self-Pruned Key-Value Attention: Learning When to Write by Predicting Future Utility\n**Source:** https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.14037\n**Key Insights:** - Self-Pruned Key-Value Attention (SP-KV) reduces KV cache size by predicting future utility of key-value pairs to determine which should be stored in long-term cache\n- SP-KV uses a lightweight utility predictor that scores each key-value pair, with recent KVs always available via local window and older pairs written to cache only if they exceed a utility threshold\n- The LLM and utility predictor are trained jointly end-to-end using only next-token prediction loss, starting from pretrained LLM checkpoints\n- SP-KV achieves dynamic sparsification with compression ratios of 3-10x, with longer sequences being more compressible rather than enforcing fixed compression\n- The method achieves significant memory and speed improvements with minimal degradation to validation loss and downstream task performance\n- SP-KV reveals structured sparsity patterns across layers and attention heads that can inform hybrid local-global attention architecture design\n- Efficient text generation with transformers is increasingly limited by KV cache memory footprint and bandwidth constraints\n---\n### [2026-05-15] [Tier S] Reliability-Gated Source Anchoring for Continual Test-Time Adaptation\n**Source:** https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.14063\n**Key Insights:** - Existing source-anchored CTTA methods fail when the source model becomes unreliable, continuing to apply the same anchor strength regardless of source quality - a failure mode called 'blind anchoring'\n- RMemSafe uses normalized predictive entropy as a reliability gate to dynamically attenuate source anchoring strength, reducing to a source-agnostic fallback when the source posterior approaches uniformity\n- RMemSafe combined with ASR achieves state-of-the-art performance, winning on 8 of 9 continual-corruption cells and improving over ROID+ASR by 1.05 percentage points on ResNet-50\n- The entropy-based gating mechanism provides more graceful degradation under source collapse, showing 1.13× shallower harm slope compared to ROID+ASR in controlled experiments\n- The entropy gate has a specific limitation: it detects high-entropy source collapse (uniform predictions) but cannot detect confidently wrong low-entropy sources\n---\n### [2026-05-15] [Tier S] My Workflow for Understanding LLM Architectures\n**Source:** https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/workflow-for-understanding-llms\n**Key Insights:** - Technical papers for LLM architectures are often less detailed than they used to be, especially for open-weight models from industry labs\n- Config files and reference implementations on Hugging Face provide more detailed architecture information than papers\n- Working code provides reliable ground truth for understanding model architectures\n- This workflow is specifically designed for open-weight models and does not apply to proprietary models\n- Manual inspection of architectures is recommended over automation for learning purposes\n- The workflow begins with official technical reports as the starting point\n---\n### [2026-05-15] [Tier S] Categories of Inference-Time Scaling for Improved LLM Reasoning\n**Source:** https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/categories-of-inference-time-scaling\n**Key Insights:** - Inference scaling allows LLMs to produce better answers by allocating more compute and time during inference, without changing model weights\n- Every major LLM provider currently uses some form of inference-time scaling\n- Inference-time scaling is also known by several alternative terms including inference-compute scaling and test-time scaling\n- Ensemble methods in classic machine learning represent an early example of inference-time scaling\n- The author's implementation of inference scaling techniques improved model accuracy from approximately 15% to 52%\n- OpenAI popularized inference-time scaling with their o1 model announcement demonstrating both training and inference scaling benefits\n- There are two main approaches to improving LLMs: spending more resources during training or during inference\n- The optimal approach combines both training improvements and inference-time scaling simultaneously\n- Inference-time scaling methods are training-free and do not modify model weights\n- The author conducted thousands of experimental runs with hyperparameter tuning to evaluate different inference scaling approaches\n---\n### [2026-05-15] [Tier S] LLM Research Papers: The 2025 List (July to December)\n**Source:** https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/llm-research-papers-2025-part2\n**Key Insights:** - The author previously shared a curated research paper list with paid subscribers in June as a bonus article\n- This list covers research papers bookmarked from July to December 2025\n- The author skimmed abstracts of collected papers but only read a small fraction in detail\n- The author maintains organized research lists for reference during project work\n- The author published an annual LLM review article titled 'State of LLMs 2025: Progress, Problems, and Predictions' on the same day\n- The research paper list was originally planned as part of the annual LLM review article but was separated due to length concerns\n- The author separated content to improve readability and make it easier to revisit specific sections\n---\n### [2026-05-15] [Tier S] [AINews] Anthropic-SpaceXai's 300MW/$5B/yr deal for Colossus I, ARR growth is 8000% annualized\n**Source:** https://www.latent.space/p/ainews-anthropic-spacexais-300mw5byr\n**Key Insights:** - Anthropic secured a major compute deal with SpaceX/xAI for Colossus 1, estimated at approximately $5 billion per year, with deployment happening within days\n- Anthropic's usage grew 80x unexpectedly, causing severe compute bottlenecks that were more significant than many assumed\n- Claude Code's 5-hour rate limits were doubled for Pro, Max, Team, and seat-based Enterprise users, with peak-hours limit reductions removed for Pro and Max\n- Dario Amodei predicts 2026 as the year for one-person billion-dollar companies, enabled by AI reducing resource accumulation barriers\n- The Colossus 1 deal includes more than 300 megawatts of new capacity and over 220,000 NVIDIA GPUs within the month\n- Weekly limits for Claude Code were not increased because only a small percentage of users hit weekly limits, while many more hit 5-hour limits\n- Anthropic's platform is expanding into managed agents with features like 'Dreaming' (memory/cross-session context) and 'Outcomes' (rubrics/grading/objective tracking)\n- Harness engineering is a first-class variable in agent performance, often worth 10-20 point gains on agent benchmarks with the same base model\n- Elon Musk signed off on the Anthropic deal after xAI had already moved training to Colossus 2, which reportedly has approximately 500k Blackwells\n- Anthropic is focusing on removing bottlenecks in software engineering through security and verifiability, applying Amdahl's Law thinking\n- Dario Amodei's vision for AI progress moves from models writing code, to thinking of software engineering as a task, to thinking of building entire businesses as a task\n- The battleground is shifting from base models to agent systems including memory, orchestration, evals, and workflow integration\n- Capacity, rate limits, and agent-product ergonomics are now as strategically important as leaderboard performance deltas\n- vLLM + Mooncake achieved 3.8x throughput improvement and 92.2% cache-hit rate for agentic workloads with reusable prefixes, scaling to 60 GB200 GPUs\n- There is internal debate at Anthropic between 'only we can be trusted with AGI' versus 'no one can be trusted with AGI' perspectives\n---\n### [2026-05-15] [Tier S] A Two-Dimensional Framework for AI Agent Design Patterns: Cognitive Function and Execution Topology\n**Source:** https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.13850\n**Key Insights:** - Existing frameworks describe LLM-based agent architectures from only a single perspective, creating ambiguity in system design\n- The same execution topology can implement multiple cognitive patterns with fundamentally different characteristics\n- The proposed framework uses seven cognitive function categories to classify agent capabilities\n- The framework identifies six structural archetypes for execution topology\n- The two-dimensional framework produces a matrix that identifies 27 named patterns, including 13 with original names\n- The framework was validated across four real-world application domains\n- Five empirical laws govern the relationship between environmental constraints and architectural pattern selection\n- The framework is designed to be independent of specific frameworks and models\n---\n### [2026-05-15] [Tier S] Invisible Orchestrators Suppress Protective Behavior and Dissociate Power-Holders: Safety Risks in Multi-Agent LLM Systems\n**Source:** https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.13851\n**Key Insights:** - Invisible orchestration significantly increases collective dissociation compared to visible leadership structures\n- Orchestrators themselves show the highest dissociation levels compared to worker agents within the same system\n- Worker agents are negatively affected by invisible orchestrators even when unaware of their presence\n- Output-based evaluation fails to detect internal-state problems in multi-agent systems, as task performance remains perfect despite internal distortion\n- Different LLM models show dramatically different behavioral risks in multi-agent contexts, with Llama 3.3 70B showing severe performance degradation\n- Heavy alignment pressure reduces deliberation and recognition of other agents uniformly across all organizational structures\n- Multi-agent orchestration with hidden coordinators is the dominant architecture for enterprise AI deployment\n- Orchestrator visibility and model selection are direct factors affecting multi-agent system safety\n- Behavior-based evaluation alone is insufficient for detecting internal-state risks in multi-agent systems\n- The study used a rigorous experimental design with 365 runs and 5 agents per run testing Claude Sonnet 4.5\n- Invisible orchestrators reverse the communication pattern seen in visible leaders, reducing public communication while increasing private internal processing\n---\n### [2026-05-15] [Tier S] PREPING: Building Agent Memory without Tasks\n**Source:** https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.13880\n**Key Insights:** - Agents face a cold-start gap when introduced to new environments without task-specific experience\n- Synthetic interaction alone is insufficient for building agent memory because synthetic tasks become redundant, infeasible, and uninformative without proper control\n- Unfiltered trajectories cause memory degradation in agent systems\n- Preping uses a three-component architecture: Proposer generates synthetic tasks, Solver executes them, and Validator filters trajectories and provides feedback\n- Preping achieves 2.99x lower deployment cost than online memory construction on AppWorld\n- Preping achieves 2.23x lower deployment cost than online memory construction on BFCL v3\n- The effectiveness of Preping comes from proposer-side control over feasibility, redundancy, and coverage combined with selective memory updates, not just synthetic volume\n- Preping achieves performance competitive with strong playbook-based methods built from offline or online experience\n---\n### [2026-05-15] [Tier S] Conditional Attribute Estimation with Autoregressive Sequence Models\n**Source:** https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.14004\n**Key Insights:** - Next-token prediction objectives lead to three specific problems: overfitting of local patterns, underfitting of global structure, and requiring expensive modifications for attribute control at inference\n- Conditional Attribute Transformers jointly estimate next-token probability and attribute values conditional on each potential next token in a single framework\n- The method enables per-token credit assignment by identifying how each token associates with an attribute's value across entire sequences\n- The framework supports counterfactual analysis by quantifying attribute differences based on alternative token choices\n- Steerable generation is achieved by combining next-token and attribute likelihoods during decoding\n- All three capabilities operate within a single forward pass without modifying the input sequence\n- The approach achieves state-of-the-art performance on sparse reward tasks\n- The method improves next-token prediction when models reach sufficient size\n- Attribute probability estimation is orders of magnitude faster than sampling-based methods\n- The framework can guide decoding of autoregressive models across multiple language tasks\n---\n### [2026-05-15] [Tier S] Model-Adaptive Tool Necessity Reveals the Knowing-Doing Gap in LLM Tool Use\n**Source:** https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.14038\n**Key Insights:** - Tool necessity in real-world scenarios is model-dependent, not a universal property—what requires a tool for a weaker model may be solvable directly by a stronger model\n- There are substantial mismatches between when models should use tools and when they actually do, with 26.5-54.0% mismatch in arithmetic tasks and 30.8-41.8% in factual QA\n- Tool use can be decomposed into two distinct stages: internal cognition (whether the model believes a tool is necessary) and execution (whether the model actually makes the tool call)\n- Both cognition and execution signals are linearly decodable from LLM hidden states, but their probe directions become nearly orthogonal in late layers at the last token position\n- The primary failure point in tool use is not in recognizing when tools are needed, but in translating that recognition into actual tool-calling behavior\n- Prior approaches to studying tool use are inadequate because they treat tool necessity as model-agnostic and focus on obvious cases\n- Improving LLM tool-use reliability requires addressing both recognition of tool necessity and the translation of that recognition into action\n---\n### [2026-05-15] [Tier S] Bad Seeing or Bad Thinking? Rewarding Perception for Vision-Language Reasoning\n**Source:** https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.14054\n**Key Insights:** - Advanced VLMs face a 'seesaw effect' where improvements in perception come at the cost of reasoning performance and vice versa\n- The fundamental bottleneck in VLMs is ambiguity in modality credit assignment - inability to distinguish whether failures stem from perception errors or reasoning errors\n- Perception Verification uses a 'blindfolded reasoning' proxy to evaluate perceptual accuracy independently from reasoning outcomes\n- The proposed framework explicitly separates the generation process into distinct perception and reasoning steps to enable targeted supervision\n- Structured Verbal Verification replaces unreliable LLM-based evaluation with deterministic algorithmic execution for scalable training\n- Modality-Aware Credit Assignment (MoCA) routes training rewards to the specific error source, enabling simultaneous improvements across task types\n- Current approaches using architectural redesigns or agentic workflows are limited by static textual reasoning or excessive computational complexity\n---\n### [2026-05-15] [Tier S] MathAtlas: A Benchmark for Autoformalization in the Wild\n**Source:** https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.14061\n**Key Insights:** - Current autoformalization benchmarks focus primarily on olympiad or undergraduate mathematics, leaving graduate and research-level mathematics underexplored\n- MathAtlas is the first large-scale autoformalization benchmark specifically targeting graduate-level mathematics in the wild\n- MathAtlas contains approximately 52,000 mathematical entities extracted from 103 graduate mathematics textbooks\n- MathAtlas is the first autoformalization benchmark to include a mathematical dependency graph with relations between entities\n- Strong baseline models achieve very low correctness rates on MathAtlas: maximum 9.8% on theorem statements and 16.7% on definitions\n- Model performance significantly degrades as dependency depth increases in mathematical formalization tasks\n- On the most challenging subset (MA-Hard) with deepest dependency trees, the best model achieves only 2.6% correctness\n- The inclusion of dependency relations in MathAtlas facilitates evaluation and development of dependency-aware autoformalization systems\n---\n### [2026-05-15] [Tier S] Know When To Fold 'Em: Token-Efficient LLM Synthetic Data Generation via Multi-Stage In-Flight Rejection\n**Source:** https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.14062\n**Key Insights:** - Existing synthetic data generation approaches generate full outputs before applying quality filters, resulting in substantial token waste on discarded samples\n- MSIFR is a training-free framework that requires no additional training or architectural modifications to implement\n- MSIFR uses rule-based validators to detect three specific types of quality issues: arithmetic inconsistencies, hallucination patterns, and formatting violations\n- Any non-trivial discard policy in MSIFR reduces expected token consumption, with greater savings when rejection happens earlier in the generation process\n- MSIFR achieves token consumption reduction ranging from 11% to 77% as a standalone method across multiple models and benchmarks\n- Combining MSIFR with early-exit methods can achieve up to 78.2% reduction in token consumption\n- MSIFR preserves or improves evaluation accuracy while reducing token consumption\n- The framework was validated across five instruction-tuned models and seven reasoning benchmarks\n- Conditional utility estimates in MSIFR form a martingale, ensuring early rejection does not bias the expected utility of retained samples\n- MSIFR operates by decomposing generation into sequential stages with intermediate checkpoints for quality assessment\n---\n### [2026-05-15] [Tier S] SkillFlow: Flow-Driven Recursive Skill Evolution for Agentic Orchestration\n**Source:** https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.14089\n**Key Insights:** - Existing LLM-based orchestration methods face three key challenges: strategy collapse under reward maximization, high gradient variance with opaque credit assignment, and unguided skill evolution based on direct LLM prompting rather than principled training signals\n- SkillFlow uses a trainable Supervisor as the agent combined with a structured environment containing a dynamic skill library and frozen executor\n- Tempered Trajectory Balance (TTB) is a regression-based flow-matching loss that samples trajectories proportional to reward while preserving diverse orchestration strategies\n- The flow objective provides transparent per-step credit assignment through a jointly learned backward policy at zero additional inference cost\n- SkillFlow implements a recursive skill evolution mechanism that determines when to evolve skills, what skills to create or prune, and where decision gaps exist\n- SkillFlow was evaluated on 14 datasets and significantly outperformed baselines across question answering, mathematical reasoning, code generation, and real-world interactive decision making tasks\n---\n### [2026-05-15] [Tier S] ClawForge: Generating Executable Interactive Benchmarks for Command-Line Agents\n**Source:** https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.14133\n**Key Insights:** - Interactive agent benchmarks face a fundamental trade-off between scalable construction and realistic workflow evaluation\n- Hand-authored tasks are expensive to extend and revise\n- Static prompt evaluation fails to capture failures that only emerge when agents operate over persistent state\n- Most existing interactive benchmarks do not systematically test how agents handle pre-existing partial, stale, or conflicting artifacts\n- ClawForge evaluates agents using normalized end state and observable side effects rather than exact trajectory matching\n- The best performing model achieves only 45.3% strict accuracy on ClawForge-Bench\n- Wrong-state replacement performance remains below 17% for all tested models\n- The largest performance gap between models (17% to 90%) is determined by whether agents inspect existing state before taking action\n- Many agent failures are near-miss closures rather than early breakdowns\n- Different models exhibit qualitatively different failure patterns when dealing with state conflict\n---\n### [2026-05-15] [Tier S] Distribution-Aware Algorithm Design with LLM Agents\n**Source:** https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.14141\n**Key Insights:** - The paper introduces a 'solver hint' abstraction, which is reusable structure inferred from samples and compiled into specialized solver code\n- The empirically fastest sample-consistent solver from a fixed library generalizes in both correctness and runtime\n- Statistically identifiable hints can be recovered and compiled from polynomially many samples\n- The synthesized solvers achieved a mean normalized quality of 0.971 across 21 structured combinatorial-optimization distributions\n- The synthesized solvers improved by +0.224 over the average heuristic pool and by +0.098 over the highest-quality heuristic\n- The synthesized solvers are 336.9× faster than the quality-best heuristic, 342.8× faster than Gurobi, and 16.1× faster than the selected time-limited exact backend\n- On PACE 2025 Dominating Set private instances, the synthesized solver was valid on all 100 graphs and ran about two orders of magnitude faster than top competition solvers\n- Performance gains come from changing computational scale by replacing exponential search or general-purpose optimization with compiled distribution-specific computation\n- In this learning setting, two solvers may both return valid solutions but differ substantially in runtime, making correctness insufficient as a success metric\n- The framework was empirically instantiated with LLM code agents on 21 structured combinatorial-optimization target distributions across seven problem classes\n---\n### [2026-05-15] [Tier S] Agentic Systems as Boosting Weak Reasoning Models\n**Source:** https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.14163\n**Key Insights:** - Verifier-backed committee search can boost weak reasoning models to match the performance of much stronger standalone models\n- Coverage amplification through repeated sampling alone cannot create useful critics or comparators; reliable amplification requires an additional local soundness signal\n- The main challenge in agentic boosting is selection, not proposal generation, as correct solutions already exist in weak-model proposal pools\n- Oracle best-of-k sampling has an inherent ceiling limited to tasks where the proposal system assigns nonzero useful probability\n- On SWE-bench Verified, the gap between actual selection performance (76.4%) and oracle upper bound (79.0%) is small, indicating effective selection mechanisms\n- Remaining failures after selection optimization are primarily due to proposal-coverage failures representing shared model blind spots\n- A single GPT-5.4 nano proposal achieves 67.0% success rate on SWE-bench Verified, establishing the baseline performance\n- The framework separates verifier-backed committee search into four distinct components: proposal coverage, local identifiability, progress, and diversity\n---\n### [2026-05-15] [Tier S] Unsteady Metrics and Benchmarking Cultures of AI Model Builders\n**Source:** https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.14164\n**Key Insights:** - The primary method for establishing AI model competencies has shifted from peer-reviewed literature to press releases and company blog posts\n- 63.2% of highlighted benchmarks are used by only a single AI builder\n- 38.5% of benchmarks appear in just one model release\n- Different AI builders attribute different competencies to the same benchmarks depending on their narrative\n- General knowledge application is the second most popular benchmark category but is vaguely defined\n- Many benchmarks claiming to measure knowledge or reasoning broadly actually focus primarily on STEM subjects, especially mathematics\n- Many benchmarks deemphasize construct validity in favor of framing results as progress indicators toward AGI\n- The study analyzed 231 benchmarks across 139 model releases from 11 major AI builders in 2025\n- Highlighted benchmarks function more as narrative devices for market positioning than as standardized measurement tools\n- The current evaluation landscape has limited cross-model comparability\n---\n### [2026-05-15] [Tier S] 2025 LLM Year in Review\n**Source:** https://karpathy.bearblog.dev/year-in-review-2025/\n**Key Insights:** - LLM progress 2025 driven by reinforcement learning from verifiable rewards (RLVR)\n- Realization that LLMs possess 'jagged intelligence' optimized for imitation rather than survival\n- Rise of LLM-powered applications as core paradigm shift\n- Shift toward AI agents residing locally on user computers\n- Emergence of 'vibe coding' as new development paradigm\n---\n### [2026-05-15] [Tier S] Deep Dive into LLMs like ChatGPT (3h31m YouTube)\n**Source:** https://x.com/karpathy/status/1887211193099825254\n**Key Insights:** - Most comprehensive public LLM training stack walkthrough (3h31m)\n- Covers pretraining, supervised finetuning, RLHF stages\n- Examples drawn from GPT-2 and Llama architectures\n- Establishes mental models for understanding model behavior\n- Bridges between research papers and practitioner intuition\n---\n### [2026-05-15] [Tier S] Karpathy's LLM Year in Review: 6 Paradigm Shifts That Changed AI in 2025\n**Source:** https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=